  "worked_solution": "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ terms and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}+54x-32y & =53 \\\\ (4{y}^{2}-32y)+(-9{x}^{2}+54x) & =53 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-8y]-9[{x}^{2}-6x] & =53 \\\\ 4[(y-4)^{2}-16]-9[(x-3)^{2}-9] & =53 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-64-9(x-3)^{2}+81 & =53 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-9(x-3)^{2} & =53+64-81 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-9(x-3)^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{4(y-4)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9(x-3)^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{36}{36} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(3,4),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-4=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-3).\n$$",
